From c340deead506bf646daefeada359026af4a5c3ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 13:59:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 204/209] feat(area): Recenter Pivot editor action with undo

The engine-side "Recenter Points" button shipped in the spline shape was wrong
for the user and is gone. It moved the curve visibly and could not be undone,
because a reflected ActionButton runs inside the component: it can change the
component's own members, and nothing else. Moving a PIVOT means moving the
entity, and in Sandbox the editor object owns the transform - CEntityObject
pushes its transform onto the entity and never reads one back (there is no
ENTITY_EVENT_XFORM handler in EntityObject.cpp). So the button compensated by
shifting the points and the Offset instead, which is not what "recenter pivot"
means to anyone, and none of it went through the undo manager.

It is now an editor action, through the generic mechanism stage 2 added: the
component declares it in IEditorActionComponent::GetEditorAction and
CEntityObject::CreateComponentWidget turns it into an SEditToolButton bound to
that component instance. ShapeEditorActions.h holds the two actions a kind with
an authored point list offers - the 1b point tool and this one - so Polygon and
Spline each declare them in one line and Box and Sphere are untouched.

CShapeRecenterTool is a one-shot: an inspector button starts a CEditTool, so the
work happens on the first Display() - not in SetUserData(), which runs while the
property tree is still installing the tool - and it then pops itself off the
level editor. It remembers every point's WORLD position, calls
CBaseObject::SetWorldPos(centroid), rewrites every point in the new local frame
from the remembered positions so that nothing moves on screen, runs the 1a sync
and accepts one CUndo. The object snapshot taken at the start covers both the
transform and the serialized points, so Ctrl+Z puts the pivot and the points
back together. A shape already centred to within a millimetre is left alone
rather than given an undo step that changes nothing.

